# Internet mail messages (RFC 5322) with MIME (RFC 2045 to 2047, RFC
# 2231): a message built from its parts, and a message read back into its
# headers, its tree of parts and their decoded bodies, written in iyi.
# Nothing here reaches the network, the clock or the disk: std/smtp sends
# what this builds.
#
#     import std/mail::{Mail, MailError}
#
#     msg = Mail::Message.new
#     msg.from = Mail::Address.new("jorg@example.com", "Jörg Müller")
#     msg.to << Mail::Address.new("ann@example.org")
#     msg.subject = "Grüße aus Köln"
#     msg.date = Mail.date(1792224000_i64, 120)
#     msg.message_id = "<20261017.1@example.com>"
#     msg.text = "Hallo Ann,\n..."
#     msg.html = "<p>Hallo Ann,</p>"
#     msg.attach("bericht.pdf", pdf_bytes)
#     wire = msg.to_s
#
#     part = Mail.parse(wire).or_panic
#     part.header("Subject")                  # "Grüße aus Köln"
#     part.addresses("From").or_panic[0].name # "Jörg Müller"
#     part.find("text/plain").not_nil         # the text, decoded
#
# **Building.** `Mail::Message` takes From, To, Cc, Bcc, Reply-To, Subject,
# the Date and the Message-ID from the caller - `Mail.date` writes a Date
# from Unix seconds and an offset, and no identifier is made up here, since
# both would need the clock or a source of randomness - and a text body, an
# HTML body, and attachments. Text alone is one `text/plain` part; text
# and HTML are `multipart/alternative`, plain first as RFC 2046 section
# 5.1.4 orders them; attachments put that body and themselves in a
# `multipart/mixed`. A text part's line breaks are written as CRLF, the
# canonical form of RFC 2046 section 4.1.1, whichever of CRLF, CR or LF
# the caller used; it goes as `7bit` when it is ASCII in lines of 78 or
# fewer, and otherwise as whichever of quoted-printable and base64 is the
# shorter, as Python's `email` chooses. Attachments go as base64. Bcc is
# not written into the message: its addresses are among `recipients`,
# where std/smtp sends to them, and no other recipient learns them.
#
# A boundary is `iyi-` and 32 hex digits of the SHA-1 of the parts it
# separates, made again from itself while a part holds it - as std/http's
# `MultipartBuilder` makes one - so it cannot be in a part by construction,
# the same message builds to the same bytes, and std/mail reaches no
# source of randomness for it.
#
# **Headers** are folded at whitespace so that every line is 78 columns or
# fewer (RFC 5322 section 2.1.1), save an address longer than that, which
# has nowhere to fold. A Subject or other unstructured value that is not
# printable ASCII in words of single spaces - non-ASCII text, a tab, space
# at either end or two together, a word too long for a line, or `=?`,
# which a reader would take for the start of an encoded-word - goes as RFC
# 2047 encoded-words: UTF-8, each word 75 characters or fewer and holding
# whole characters, in Q or B, whichever is the shorter. A display name of
# atoms goes as it is, one with other printable ASCII as a quoted string,
# and any other as encoded-words - one word when it fits one, and its
# atoms between words when it does not, since Python's `email` reads a
# space into the gap between two encoded-words of a name, where RFC 2047
# section 6.2 drops it (its own long names come back so); an attachment's
# file name that is not
# short printable ASCII goes as RFC 2231 `filename*`, in continuations
# when it is long. A header value or a display name that holds a line
# break is a panic, as it would be a header of the caller's making.
#
# **Reading.** `Mail.parse` reads a message whose lines end in CRLF or LF
# into a `Mail::Part`: its fields unfolded, in order, and its body; a
# multipart's parts, and a `message/rfc822`'s message, are parts of their
# own. `header` answers a field with its encoded-words decoded; `body` the
# bytes the transfer encoding stands for; `text` those bytes in UTF-8 from
# the part's charset - UTF-8, US-ASCII, ISO-8859-1, ISO-8859-15 and
# windows-1252, under their usual names - and an error that names any
# other. `addresses` reads an address list: RFC 5322's mailboxes and
# groups (whose members it answers, the group's name set aside), quoted
# local parts, domain literals, and RFC 6532's UTF-8. Comments are passed
# over wherever whitespace may be, and are never taken for a name: the
# old `addr (Name)` form is an address without one. A route in an angle
# address (obs-route) is passed over too.
#
# Where RFC 2045 asks a reader to be forgiving, it is: a base64 body's
# characters outside the alphabet are passed over and it ends at its
# padding (section 6.8), and an `=` in quoted-printable that starts no
# escape is kept (section 6.7, note 2); an encoded-word that does not
# decode is left as it was written (RFC 2047 section 6.3), and a
# Content-Type that cannot be read is `text/plain` (RFC 2045 section 5.2).
# What is refused, as a `MailError`, is what leaves the structure in doubt:
# a header line that is not a field, a header section that begins with a
# folded line, a multipart that names no boundary, has no boundary line or
# ends before its closing one, parts nested deeper than 32, and an address
# list that is not one. A body in a transfer encoding that is not one of
# RFC 2045's is an error when it is asked for, and so is text not in its
# charset.
#
# Quoted-printable is written as Python's `email.quoprimime.body_encode`
# writes it, line for line, and read as RFC 2045 says; base64 in lines of
# 76. `bench/std_mail_exercise.sh` has Python's `email` read what this
# builds and this read what Python's `EmailMessage` builds, under
# `policy.default` and `policy.SMTP`, holds the encodings to `quopri`,
# `base64` and `email.quoprimime`, and dates to `email.utils`.
#
# Not here: S/MIME and PGP, DKIM, `format=flowed`, charsets beyond the
# five, and writing 8-bit bodies: everything this writes is 7-bit but for
# an RFC 6532 address, which `Message#utf8?` names for SMTPUTF8.

module std/mail

import std/digest::{SHA1}
import std/base64::{Base64}
import std/mime::{MIME}
import std/text

# What a message, a header or an address is that it should not be, as a
# value: `reason` says what; `message` says it is mail's.
pub struct MailError
  getter reason : String

  def initialize(@reason : String)
  end

  def to_s : String
    "mail: " + @reason
  end
end

impl Error for MailError
  def message : String
    "mail: " + @reason
  end
end

# Bytes, characters and charsets: what the rest is written over.
struct MailText
  HEX = "0123456789ABCDEF"

  # windows-1252's 0x80 to 0x9F, -1 where it defines nothing.
  CP1252 = [0x20AC, -1, 0x201A, 0x0192, 0x201E, 0x2026, 0x2020, 0x2021,
            0x02C6, 0x2030, 0x0160, 0x2039, 0x0152, -1, 0x017D, -1,
            -1, 0x2018, 0x2019, 0x201C, 0x201D, 0x2022, 0x2013, 0x2014,
            0x02DC, 0x2122, 0x0161, 0x203A, 0x0153, -1, 0x017E, 0x0178]

  def self.hex_value(b : UInt8) : Int32
    return b.to_i32 - 48 if b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8
    return b.to_i32 - 55 if b >= 65_u8 && b <= 70_u8
    return b.to_i32 - 87 if b >= 97_u8 && b <= 102_u8
    -1
  end

  def self.wsp?(b : UInt8) : Bool
    b == 32_u8 || b == 9_u8
  end

  def self.cut(text : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32) : String
    stop > start ? text[start, stop - start] : ""
  end

  # `=` and the byte in two upper-case hex digits.
  def self.escape(io : String::Builder, b : UInt8, mark : UInt8 = 61_u8) : Nil
    io.write_byte(mark)
    io.write_byte(HEX.to_unsafe[b.to_i32 >> 4])
    io.write_byte(HEX.to_unsafe[b.to_i32 & 15])
  end

  def self.ascii?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return false if p[i] >= 128_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  def self.line_break?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return true if p[i] == 13_u8 || p[i] == 10_u8 || p[i] == 0_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    false
  end

  # Whether *text* is well-formed UTF-8: no overlong form, no surrogate,
  # nothing past U+10FFFF.
  def self.utf8?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    i = 0
    while i < n
      b = p[i].to_i32
      if b < 128
        i = i + 1
      else
        need = 0
        cp = 0
        least = 0
        if b >= 0xC2 && b <= 0xDF
          need = 1
          cp = b & 0x1F
          least = 0x80
        elsif b >= 0xE0 && b <= 0xEF
          need = 2
          cp = b & 0x0F
          least = 0x800
        elsif b >= 0xF0 && b <= 0xF4
          need = 3
          cp = b & 0x07
          least = 0x10000
        else
          return false
        end
        return false if i + need >= n
        k = 1
        while k <= need
          c = p[i + k].to_i32
          return false unless (c & 0xC0) == 0x80
          cp = (cp << 6) | (c & 0x3F)
          k = k + 1
        end
        return false if cp < least || cp > 0x10FFFF || (cp >= 0xD800 && cp <= 0xDFFF)
        i = i + need + 1
      end
    end
    true
  end

  # The bytes of the character at *i*: its lead byte's count, one for a
  # byte that leads none, never past the end.
  def self.char_size(p : Pointer(UInt8), i : Int32, n : Int32) : Int32
    b = p[i]
    size = 1
    if b >= 0xF0_u8 && b <= 0xF7_u8
      size = 4
    elsif b >= 0xE0_u8
      size = 3
    elsif b >= 0xC0_u8
      size = 2
    end
    size = 1 if b >= 0xF8_u8
    size > n - i ? n - i : size
  end

  def self.append_utf8(io : String::Builder, cp : Int32) : Nil
    if cp < 0x80
      io.write_byte(cp.to_u8)
    elsif cp < 0x800
      io.write_byte((0xC0 | (cp >> 6)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | (cp & 0x3F)).to_u8)
    elsif cp < 0x10000
      io.write_byte((0xE0 | (cp >> 12)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | (cp & 0x3F)).to_u8)
    else
      io.write_byte((0xF0 | (cp >> 18)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3F)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F)).to_u8)
      io.write_byte((0x80 | (cp & 0x3F)).to_u8)
    end
  end

  # The charset *name* stands for, by IANA's names and their aliases in
  # use, or nil for one this does not read.
  def self.charset(name : String) : String?
    case name.strip.downcase
    when "utf-8", "utf8"
      "utf-8"
    when "us-ascii", "ascii", "us", "ansi_x3.4-1968", "iso646-us"
      "us-ascii"
    when "iso-8859-1", "iso8859-1", "iso_8859-1", "latin1", "latin-1", "l1", "cp819", "iso-ir-100"
      "iso-8859-1"
    when "iso-8859-15", "iso8859-15", "iso_8859-15", "latin-9", "latin9"
      "iso-8859-15"
    when "windows-1252", "cp1252"
      "windows-1252"
    else
      nil
    end
  end

  # *data*, in *name*'s charset, as UTF-8.
  def self.decode(data : String, name : String) : String | MailError
    kind = MailText.charset(name)
    return MailError.new("the charset #{name.inspect} is not one std/mail reads (UTF-8, US-ASCII, ISO-8859-1, ISO-8859-15, windows-1252)") unless kind
    p = data.to_unsafe
    n = data.bytesize
    if kind == "utf-8"
      return data if MailText.utf8?(data)
      return MailError.new("text said to be UTF-8 is not UTF-8")
    end
    i = 0
    if kind == "us-ascii"
      while i < n
        return MailError.new("text said to be US-ASCII holds the byte 0x#{p[i].to_i32.to_s(16)}") if p[i] >= 128_u8
        i = i + 1
      end
      return data
    end
    io = String::Builder.new(n + n // 4 + 1)
    while i < n
      b = p[i].to_i32
      if b < 128
        io.write_byte(p[i])
      else
        cp = b
        if kind == "windows-1252" && b < 0xA0
          cp = CP1252[b - 128]
          return MailError.new("text said to be windows-1252 holds the byte 0x#{b.to_s(16)}, which it leaves undefined") if cp < 0
        elsif kind == "iso-8859-15"
          cp = MailText.latin9(b)
        end
        MailText.append_utf8(io, cp)
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # ISO-8859-15's eight differences from ISO-8859-1.
  def self.latin9(b : Int32) : Int32
    case b
    when 0xA4 then 0x20AC
    when 0xA6 then 0x0160
    when 0xA8 then 0x0161
    when 0xB4 then 0x017D
    when 0xB8 then 0x017E
    when 0xBC then 0x0152
    when 0xBD then 0x0153
    when 0xBE then 0x0178
    else           b
    end
  end

  # *text* with CRLF, CR and LF each a CRLF.
  def self.crlf(text : String) : String
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n + n // 16 + 2)
    i = 0
    while i < n
      b = p[i]
      if b == 13_u8
        io.write_byte(13_u8)
        io.write_byte(10_u8)
        i = i + 1 if i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
      elsif b == 10_u8
        io.write_byte(13_u8)
        io.write_byte(10_u8)
      else
        io.write_byte(b)
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # *text* with each CRLF an LF.
  def self.lf(text : String) : String
    return text unless text.includes?("\r\n")
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n)
    i = 0
    while i < n
      io.write_byte(p[i]) unless p[i] == 13_u8 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # --- Base64 (RFC 2045 section 6.8) ---

  def self.b64_value(b : UInt8) : Int32
    return b.to_i32 - 65 if b >= 65_u8 && b <= 90_u8
    return b.to_i32 - 71 if b >= 97_u8 && b <= 122_u8
    return b.to_i32 + 4 if b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8
    return 62 if b == 43_u8
    return 63 if b == 47_u8
    -1
  end

  def self.base64_encode(data : String) : String
    flat = Base64.encode(data)
    n = flat.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n + (n // 76 + 1) * 2)
    p = flat.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < n
      take = n - i < 76 ? n - i : 76
      io.write(p + i, take)
      io << "\r\n"
      i = i + take
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # Characters outside the alphabet are passed over, as section 6.8 has
  # it, and the first `=` ends the data; bits short of a byte at the end
  # are dropped.
  def self.base64_decode(text : String) : String
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n * 3 // 4 + 4)
    acc = 0
    bits = 0
    i = 0
    while i < n
      b = p[i]
      break if b == 61_u8
      v = MailText.b64_value(b)
      if v >= 0
        acc = ((acc << 6) | v) & 0xFFFFFF
        bits = bits + 6
        if bits >= 8
          bits = bits - 8
          io.write_byte(((acc >> bits) & 0xFF).to_u8)
        end
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # --- Quoted-printable (RFC 2045 section 6.7) ---

  # Line breaks of *data* (CRLF, CR or LF) are hard breaks written as
  # CRLF, unless *binary*, when CR and LF are bytes like any other and
  # escaped. The wrapping is `email.quoprimime.body_encode`'s: a line of
  # more than 76 is cut at 75 and a soft break, never inside an escape;
  # a space or tab at a line's end is escaped, or kept before a soft
  # break where there is no room for its escape.
  def self.qp_encode(data : String, binary : Bool) : String
    p = data.to_unsafe
    n = data.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n + n // 8 + 8)
    line = String::Builder.new(128)
    i = 0
    while i < n
      line = String::Builder.new(128)
      while i < n
        b = p[i]
        break if !binary && (b == 13_u8 || b == 10_u8)
        if (b >= 32_u8 && b <= 126_u8 && b != 61_u8) || b == 9_u8
          line.write_byte(b)
        else
          MailText.escape(line, b)
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
      MailText.qp_wrap(io, line.to_s)
      if i < n
        i = i + 1 if p[i] == 13_u8 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
        i = i + 1
        io << "\r\n"
      end
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # One encoded line, cut into lines of 76 or fewer.
  def self.qp_wrap(io : String::Builder, e : String) : Nil
    p = e.to_unsafe
    size = e.bytesize
    start = 0
    last_start = size - 1 - 76
    while start <= last_start
      stop = start + 75
      if p[stop - 2] == 61_u8
        io.write(p + start, stop - 1 - start)
        start = stop - 2
      elsif p[stop - 1] == 61_u8
        io.write(p + start, stop - start)
        start = stop - 1
      else
        io.write(p + start, stop - start)
        io.write_byte(61_u8)
        start = stop
      end
      io << "\r\n"
    end
    if size > 0 && MailText.wsp?(p[size - 1])
      room = start - last_start
      io.write(p + start, size - 1 - start)
      if room >= 3
        MailText.escape(io, p[size - 1])
      elsif room == 2
        io.write_byte(p[size - 1])
        io << "=\r\n"
      else
        io << "=\r\n"
        MailText.escape(io, p[size - 1])
      end
    else
      io.write(p + start, size - start)
    end
  end

  # Hard breaks come out as CRLF. Space and tab at a line's end are
  # dropped first (rule 3: a gateway may have added them), a line's last
  # `=` is a soft break, `=` and two hex digits - either case - a byte,
  # and any other `=` is itself.
  def self.qp_decode(text : String) : String
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n)
    i = 0
    while i < n
      j = i
      while j < n && p[j] != 10_u8
        j = j + 1
      end
      e = j
      e = e - 1 if j < n && e > i && p[e - 1] == 13_u8
      while e > i && MailText.wsp?(p[e - 1])
        e = e - 1
      end
      soft = false
      k = i
      while k < e
        b = p[k]
        if b != 61_u8
          io.write_byte(b)
          k = k + 1
        elsif k + 1 == e
          soft = true
          k = k + 1
        elsif k + 2 < e && MailText.hex_value(p[k + 1]) >= 0 && MailText.hex_value(p[k + 2]) >= 0
          io.write_byte((MailText.hex_value(p[k + 1]) * 16 + MailText.hex_value(p[k + 2])).to_u8)
          k = k + 3
        else
          io.write_byte(b)
          k = k + 1
        end
      end
      io << "\r\n" if j < n && !soft
      i = j + 1
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # --- Encoded-words (RFC 2047) ---

  # Bytes a Q encoded-word may carry as they are, in any of the places
  # one may stand (section 5, rule 3); a space is `_`.
  def self.q_safe?(b : UInt8) : Bool
    (b >= 65_u8 && b <= 90_u8) || (b >= 97_u8 && b <= 122_u8) || (b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8) ||
      b == 33_u8 || b == 42_u8 || b == 43_u8 || b == 45_u8 || b == 47_u8 || b == 32_u8
  end

  def self.q_size(p : Pointer(UInt8), start : Int32, stop : Int32) : Int32
    size = 0
    i = start
    while i < stop
      size = size + (MailText.q_safe?(p[i]) ? 1 : 3)
      i = i + 1
    end
    size
  end

  # Where the characters from *i* that fit in an encoded-word's *room*
  # bytes of text end: *i* when not even the first does.
  def self.word_end(p : Pointer(UInt8), i : Int32, n : Int32, room : Int32, q : Bool) : Int32
    j = i
    size = 0
    while j < n
      c = MailText.char_size(p, j, n)
      grown = q ? size + MailText.q_size(p, j, j + c) : ((j + c - i) + 2) // 3 * 4
      break if grown > room
      size = grown
      j = j + c
    end
    j
  end

  def self.word(text : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32, q : Bool) : String
    io = String::Builder.new(80)
    io << (q ? "=?utf-8?q?" : "=?utf-8?b?")
    if q
      p = text.to_unsafe
      i = start
      while i < stop
        b = p[i]
        if b == 32_u8
          io.write_byte(95_u8)
        elsif MailText.q_safe?(b)
          io.write_byte(b)
        else
          MailText.escape(io, b)
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
    else
      io << Base64.encode(MailText.cut(text, start, stop))
    end
    io << "?="
    io.to_s
  end

  # *text* with its encoded-words decoded and the whitespace between two
  # of them dropped (section 6.2). A word is decoded where whitespace or
  # the ends set it apart, several written together among them; one that
  # does not decode is left as written.
  def self.decode_words(text : String) : String
    return text unless text.includes?("=?")
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n)
    i = 0
    after_word = false
    while i < n
      j = i
      while j < n && (MailText.wsp?(p[j]) || p[j] == 13_u8 || p[j] == 10_u8)
        j = j + 1
      end
      k = j
      while k < n && !(MailText.wsp?(p[k]) || p[k] == 13_u8 || p[k] == 10_u8)
        k = k + 1
      end
      if k == j
        io.write(p + i, j - i)
        i = k
      else
        decoded = MailText.words_in(text, j, k)
        if decoded
          io.write(p + i, j - i) unless after_word
          io << decoded
          after_word = true
        else
          io.write(p + i, k - i)
          after_word = false
        end
        i = k
      end
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # The encoded-words that are the whole of `text[start, stop)`, decoded,
  # or nil when it is anything else.
  def self.words_in(text : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32) : String?
    io = String::Builder.new(stop - start)
    at = start
    while at < stop
      one = MailText.word_at(text, at, stop)
      return nil unless one
      io << one[0]
      at = one[1]
    end
    at == start ? nil : io.to_s
  end

  def self.word_at(text : String, at : Int32, stop : Int32) : {String, Int32}?
    p = text.to_unsafe
    return nil unless at + 1 < stop && p[at] == 61_u8 && p[at + 1] == 63_u8
    c0 = at + 2
    c1 = c0
    while c1 < stop && p[c1] != 63_u8
      c1 = c1 + 1
    end
    return nil if c1 == c0 || c1 + 2 >= stop || p[c1 + 2] != 63_u8
    enc = p[c1 + 1]
    t0 = c1 + 3
    t1 = t0
    while t1 < stop && p[t1] != 63_u8
      t1 = t1 + 1
    end
    return nil unless t1 + 1 < stop && p[t1 + 1] == 61_u8
    charset = MailText.cut(text, c0, c1)
    star = charset.index('*')
    charset = charset[0, star] if star
    raw = nil.as(String?)
    if enc == 81_u8 || enc == 113_u8
      raw = MailText.q_decode(text, t0, t1)
    elsif enc == 66_u8 || enc == 98_u8
      raw = MailText.b_decode(text, t0, t1)
    end
    return nil unless raw
    decoded = MailText.decode(raw, charset)
    return nil if decoded.is_a?(MailError)
    {decoded, t1 + 2}
  end

  def self.q_decode(text : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32) : String?
    p = text.to_unsafe
    io = String::Builder.new(stop - start)
    i = start
    while i < stop
      b = p[i]
      if b == 95_u8
        io.write_byte(32_u8)
        i = i + 1
      elsif b == 61_u8
        return nil unless i + 2 < stop
        hi = MailText.hex_value(p[i + 1])
        lo = MailText.hex_value(p[i + 2])
        return nil if hi < 0 || lo < 0
        io.write_byte((hi * 16 + lo).to_u8)
        i = i + 3
      else
        io.write_byte(b)
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # The alphabet, then at most two `=` at the end: missing padding is
  # taken, as readers take it.
  def self.b_decode(text : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32) : String?
    p = text.to_unsafe
    e = stop
    pads = 0
    while e > start && p[e - 1] == 61_u8 && pads < 2
      e = e - 1
      pads = pads + 1
    end
    i = start
    while i < e
      return nil if MailText.b64_value(p[i]) < 0
      i = i + 1
    end
    return nil if (e - start) % 4 == 1
    MailText.base64_decode(MailText.cut(text, start, e))
  end

  # --- RFC 5322's lexical tokens ---

  def self.atext?(b : UInt8) : Bool
    return true if (b >= 65_u8 && b <= 90_u8) || (b >= 97_u8 && b <= 122_u8) || (b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8)
    return true if b >= 128_u8
    case b
    when 33_u8, 35_u8, 36_u8, 37_u8, 38_u8, 39_u8, 42_u8, 43_u8, 45_u8, 47_u8, 61_u8, 63_u8, 94_u8, 95_u8, 96_u8, 123_u8, 124_u8, 125_u8, 126_u8
      true
    else
      false
    end
  end

  # RFC 2045's token: printable ASCII but tspecials.
  def self.token?(b : UInt8) : Bool
    return false if b <= 32_u8 || b >= 127_u8
    case b
    when 40_u8, 41_u8, 60_u8, 62_u8, 64_u8, 44_u8, 59_u8, 58_u8, 92_u8, 34_u8, 47_u8, 91_u8, 93_u8, 63_u8, 61_u8
      false
    else
      true
    end
  end

  # *local* as an addr-spec writes it: as it is when it is a dot-atom,
  # quoted otherwise.
  def self.quote_local(local : String) : String
    p = local.to_unsafe
    n = local.bytesize
    plain = n > 0 && p[0] != 46_u8 && p[n - 1] != 46_u8
    i = 0
    while plain && i < n
      b = p[i]
      plain = false unless MailText.atext?(b) || (b == 46_u8 && p[i + 1] != 46_u8)
      i = i + 1
    end
    plain ? local : MailText.quoted(local)
  end

  def self.quoted(text : String) : String
    io = String::Builder.new(text.bytesize + 2)
    io.write_byte(34_u8)
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      io.write_byte(92_u8) if p[i] == 34_u8 || p[i] == 92_u8
      io.write_byte(p[i])
      i = i + 1
    end
    io.write_byte(34_u8)
    io.to_s
  end

  # --- MIME parameters (RFC 2045 section 5.1, RFC 2231) ---

  # A Content-Type's `type/subtype` in lower case, and its parameters;
  # nil when it is not one.
  def self.content_type(value : String) : {String, Hash(String, String)}?
    scan = MailScan.new(value)
    return nil if scan.skip_cfws
    major = scan.token
    return nil if major.empty? || scan.skip_cfws || scan.peek != 47_u8
    scan.advance
    return nil if scan.skip_cfws
    minor = scan.token
    return nil if minor.empty?
    {major.downcase + "/" + minor.downcase, MailText.params(scan)}
  end

  # A Content-Disposition's kind in lower case, and its parameters.
  def self.disposition(value : String) : {String, Hash(String, String)}?
    scan = MailScan.new(value)
    return nil if scan.skip_cfws
    kind = scan.token
    return nil if kind.empty?
    {kind.downcase, MailText.params(scan)}
  end

  # `; name=value` after `scan`, names in lower case, RFC 2231's extended
  # values and continuations put together. A parameter that cannot be read
  # ends the list; what came before it is kept.
  def self.params(scan : MailScan) : Hash(String, String)
    raw = [] of {String, String}
    while true
      break if scan.skip_cfws
      break if scan.done? || scan.peek != 59_u8
      scan.advance
      break if scan.skip_cfws
      name = scan.token.downcase
      break if name.empty? || scan.skip_cfws || scan.peek != 61_u8
      scan.advance
      break if scan.skip_cfws
      if scan.peek == 34_u8
        value = scan.quoted
        break if value.is_a?(MailError)
        raw << {name, value}
      else
        # Unquoted values with tspecials in them - `boundary=----=_Part`
        # - are what mailers send; up to `;` or whitespace is the value.
        raw << {name, scan.bare_value}
      end
    end
    MailText.assemble(raw)
  end

  def self.assemble(raw : Array({String, String})) : Hash(String, String)
    result = {} of String => String
    plain = {} of String => String
    pieces = {} of String => Array({Int32, String, Bool})
    raw.each do |pair|
      name = pair[0]
      value = pair[1]
      star = name.index('*')
      if star.nil?
        plain[name] = value if plain[name]?.nil?
      else
        base = name[0, star]
        rest = MailText.cut(name, star + 1, name.bytesize)
        list = pieces[base]? || [] of {Int32, String, Bool}
        if rest.empty?
          list << {-1, value, true}
        else
          extended = rest.ends_with?("*")
          digits = extended ? rest[0, rest.bytesize - 1] : rest
          index = digits.to_i?
          if index && index >= 0 && (digits == "0" || !digits.starts_with?("0"))
            list << {index, value, extended}
          end
        end
        pieces[base] = list
      end
    end
    pieces.each do |base, list|
      joined = MailText.continued(list)
      result[base] = joined if joined
    end
    plain.each do |name, value|
      result[name] = MailText.whole_words(value) if result[name]?.nil?
    end
    result
  end

  # A value that is encoded-words and nothing else - how some mailers
  # write a file name - decoded; any other as it is.
  def self.whole_words(value : String) : String
    return value unless value.starts_with?("=?") && value.ends_with?("?=")
    decoded = MailText.decode_words(value)
    decoded
  end

  def self.continued(list : Array({Int32, String, Bool})) : String?
    list.each do |piece|
      return MailText.extended(piece[1], true) if piece[0] == -1
    end
    sorted = list.sorted_by { |piece| piece[0] }
    bytes = String::Builder.new(64)
    charset = ""
    expect = 0
    sorted.each do |piece|
      if piece[0] == expect
        if piece[2]
          value = piece[1]
          if expect == 0
            first = value.index('\'')
            second = first ? value.index('\'', first + 1) : nil
            if first && second
              charset = value[0, first]
              value = MailText.cut(value, second + 1, value.bytesize)
            end
          end
          bytes << MailText.percent_decode(value)
        else
          bytes << piece[1]
        end
        expect = expect + 1
      end
    end
    return nil if expect == 0
    MailText.in_charset(bytes.to_s, charset)
  end

  # `charset'language'%XX...`, or with *prefixed* false just the
  # percent-encoded bytes.
  def self.extended(value : String, prefixed : Bool) : String
    charset = ""
    text = value
    first = value.index('\'')
    second = first ? value.index('\'', first + 1) : nil
    if prefixed && first && second
      charset = value[0, first]
      text = MailText.cut(value, second + 1, value.bytesize)
    end
    MailText.in_charset(MailText.percent_decode(text), charset)
  end

  # Bytes from RFC 2231's charset in UTF-8; as they are when the charset
  # is none or not one this reads.
  def self.in_charset(bytes : String, charset : String) : String
    return bytes if charset.empty?
    decoded = MailText.decode(bytes, charset)
    decoded.is_a?(MailError) ? bytes : decoded
  end

  def self.percent_decode(text : String) : String
    return text unless text.includes?("%")
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    io = String::Builder.new(n)
    i = 0
    while i < n
      if p[i] == 37_u8 && i + 2 < n && MailText.hex_value(p[i + 1]) >= 0 && MailText.hex_value(p[i + 2]) >= 0
        io.write_byte((MailText.hex_value(p[i + 1]) * 16 + MailText.hex_value(p[i + 2])).to_u8)
        i = i + 3
      else
        io.write_byte(p[i])
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # RFC 2231's attribute-char: what goes unescaped in an extended value.
  def self.attr_char?(b : UInt8) : Bool
    return true if (b >= 65_u8 && b <= 90_u8) || (b >= 97_u8 && b <= 122_u8) || (b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8)
    case b
    when 33_u8, 35_u8, 36_u8, 38_u8, 43_u8, 45_u8, 46_u8, 94_u8, 95_u8, 96_u8, 124_u8, 126_u8
      true
    else
      false
    end
  end

  # --- Dates (RFC 5322 section 3.3) ---

  WEEKDAYS  = ["Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun", "Mon", "Tue", "Wed"]
  MONTHS    = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"]
  DAY_NAMES = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun"]

  # The obsolete zones of RFC 5322 section 4.3, in minutes.
  ZONES = {"ut" => 0, "gmt" => 0, "z" => 0, "edt" => -240, "est" => -300, "cdt" => -300, "cst" => -360,
           "mdt" => -360, "mst" => -420, "pdt" => -420, "pst" => -480}

  def self.two(n : Int32) : String
    n < 10 ? "0" + n.to_s : n.to_s
  end

  # Year, month and day of *days* since 1970-01-01: Howard Hinnant's
  # civil_from_days, which is written for division that truncates, as
  # iyi's does.
  def self.civil(days : Int64) : {Int32, Int32, Int32}
    z = days + 719468_i64
    era = (z >= 0 ? z : z - 146096_i64) // 146097_i64
    doe = z - era * 146097_i64
    yoe = (doe - doe // 1460_i64 + doe // 36524_i64 - doe // 146096_i64) // 365_i64
    doy = doe - (365_i64 * yoe + yoe // 4_i64 - yoe // 100_i64)
    mp = (5_i64 * doy + 2_i64) // 153_i64
    d = doy - (153_i64 * mp + 2_i64) // 5_i64 + 1_i64
    m = mp < 10 ? mp + 3 : mp - 9
    y = yoe + era * 400_i64 + (m <= 2 ? 1_i64 : 0_i64)
    {y.to_i32, m.to_i32, d.to_i32}
  end

  def self.days_from_civil(year : Int32, month : Int32, day : Int32) : Int64
    y = (month <= 2 ? year - 1 : year).to_i64
    era = (y >= 0 ? y : y - 399_i64) // 400_i64
    yoe = y - era * 400_i64
    mp = (month > 2 ? month - 3 : month + 9).to_i64
    doy = (153_i64 * mp + 2_i64) // 5_i64 + day.to_i64 - 1_i64
    doe = yoe * 365_i64 + yoe // 4_i64 - yoe // 100_i64 + doy
    era * 146097_i64 + doe - 719468_i64
  end

  def self.month_days(year : Int32, month : Int32) : Int32
    return 29 if month == 2 && year % 4 == 0 && (year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0)
    [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31][month - 1]
  end

  def self.check_field(name : String, value : String) : Nil
    raise "mail: a header name is printable ASCII without a colon, not #{name.inspect}" unless MailText.field_name?(name)
    raise "mail: the #{name} header holds a line break" if MailText.line_break?(value)
    raise "mail: the #{name} header is not UTF-8" unless MailText.utf8?(value)
  end

  def self.field_name?(name : String) : Bool
    return false if name.empty?
    p = name.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < name.bytesize
      return false if p[i] < 33_u8 || p[i] > 126_u8 || p[i] == 58_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end
end

# A cursor over a structured header value: whitespace and comments
# passed over, tokens and quoted strings read.
class MailScan
  getter at : Int32
  @text : String
  @p : Pointer(UInt8)
  @n : Int32

  def initialize(@text : String, @at : Int32 = 0)
    @p = @text.to_unsafe
    @n = @text.bytesize
  end

  def done? : Bool
    @at >= @n
  end

  def peek : UInt8
    @at < @n ? @p[@at] : 0_u8
  end

  def advance : Nil
    @at = @at + 1
  end

  # Whether anything - whitespace or a comment - was passed over: an error
  # for a comment never closed.
  def skip_cfws : MailError?
    while @at < @n
      b = @p[@at]
      if b == 32_u8 || b == 9_u8 || b == 13_u8 || b == 10_u8
        @at = @at + 1
      elsif b == 40_u8
        depth = 0
        while true
          return MailError.new("a comment is not closed in #{@text.inspect}") if @at >= @n
          c = @p[@at]
          if c == 92_u8
            @at = @at + 1
          elsif c == 40_u8
            depth = depth + 1
          elsif c == 41_u8
            depth = depth - 1
          end
          @at = @at + 1
          break if depth == 0
        end
      else
        break
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  def token : String
    start = @at
    while @at < @n && MailText.token?(@p[@at])
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
  end

  def atom : String
    start = @at
    while @at < @n && MailText.atext?(@p[@at])
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
  end

  def digits : String
    start = @at
    while @at < @n && @p[@at] >= 48_u8 && @p[@at] <= 57_u8
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
  end

  def letters : String
    start = @at
    while @at < @n && ((@p[@at] >= 65_u8 && @p[@at] <= 90_u8) || (@p[@at] >= 97_u8 && @p[@at] <= 122_u8))
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
  end

  def bare_value : String
    start = @at
    while @at < @n && @p[@at] != 59_u8 && @p[@at] > 32_u8 && @p[@at] != 40_u8
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
  end

  # The quoted string at `"`, its quoted-pairs resolved.
  def quoted : String | MailError
    @at = @at + 1
    io = String::Builder.new(32)
    while @at < @n
      b = @p[@at]
      if b == 34_u8
        @at = @at + 1
        return io.to_s
      elsif b == 92_u8
        @at = @at + 1
        break if @at >= @n
        io.write_byte(@p[@at])
      elsif b != 13_u8 && b != 10_u8
        io.write_byte(b)
      end
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailError.new("a quoted string is not closed in #{@text.inspect}")
  end

  # A domain literal at `[`, brackets kept.
  def literal : String | MailError
    start = @at
    @at = @at + 1
    while @at < @n
      b = @p[@at]
      if b == 93_u8
        @at = @at + 1
        return MailText.cut(@text, start, @at)
      elsif b == 92_u8
        @at = @at + 1
      elsif b == 91_u8
        break
      end
      @at = @at + 1
    end
    MailError.new("a domain literal is not closed in #{@text.inspect}")
  end
end

# One word of a phrase or a local part as it was read: an atom, a quoted
# string or a dot, and whether whitespace came before it.
struct MailWord
  getter text : String
  getter kind : Int32
  getter spaced : Bool

  ATOM   = 0
  QUOTED = 1
  DOT    = 2

  def initialize(@text : String, @kind : Int32, @spaced : Bool)
  end
end

# RFC 5322 section 3.4's address lists, read.
class MailAddresses
  @scan : MailScan
  @text : String

  def initialize(@text : String)
    @scan = MailScan.new(@text)
  end

  def fail(why : String) : MailError
    MailError.new("#{why} in #{@text.inspect}")
  end

  # The mailboxes of an address list, a group's members among them.
  def list : Array(Std::Mail::Mail::Address) | MailError
    found = [] of Std::Mail::Mail::Address
    while true
      problem = @scan.skip_cfws
      return problem if problem
      break if @scan.done?
      if @scan.peek == 44_u8
        @scan.advance
      else
        item = entry(found, false)
        return item if item
        problem = @scan.skip_cfws
        return problem if problem
        break if @scan.done?
        return fail("an address is followed by #{@scan.peek.unsafe_chr.inspect}, not a comma") unless @scan.peek == 44_u8
        @scan.advance
      end
    end
    found
  end

  # One mailbox, or a group of them, added to *found*.
  def entry(found : Array(Std::Mail::Mail::Address), grouped : Bool) : MailError?
    words = phrase
    return words if words.is_a?(MailError)
    problem = @scan.skip_cfws
    return problem if problem
    case @scan.peek
    when 60_u8
      @scan.advance
      problem = @scan.skip_cfws
      return problem if problem
      if @scan.peek == 64_u8
        # obs-route: `<@a.example,@b.example:user@c.example>`, passed over.
        while !@scan.done? && @scan.peek != 58_u8
          @scan.advance
        end
        return fail("a route in an angle address does not end in a colon") if @scan.done?
        @scan.advance
      end
      spec = addr_spec(phrase_words: nil)
      return spec if spec.is_a?(MailError)
      problem = @scan.skip_cfws
      return problem if problem
      return fail("an angle address is not closed with >") unless @scan.peek == 62_u8
      @scan.advance
      found << Std::Mail::Mail::Address.new(spec[0], spec[1], MailAddresses.display(words), true)
    when 58_u8
      return fail("a group is inside a group") if grouped
      return fail("a group has no name") if words.empty?
      @scan.advance
      while true
        problem = @scan.skip_cfws
        return problem if problem
        return fail("a group is not closed with a semicolon") if @scan.done?
        c = @scan.peek
        if c == 59_u8
          @scan.advance
          break
        elsif c == 44_u8
          @scan.advance
        else
          item = entry(found, true)
          return item if item
          problem = @scan.skip_cfws
          return problem if problem
          c = @scan.peek
          return fail("a group's member is followed by neither a comma nor a semicolon") unless c == 44_u8 || c == 59_u8
        end
      end
    when 64_u8
      spec = addr_spec(phrase_words: words)
      return spec if spec.is_a?(MailError)
      found << Std::Mail::Mail::Address.new(spec[0], spec[1], "", true)
    else
      return fail("a name with no address") unless words.empty?
      return fail("#{@scan.done? ? "nothing" : @scan.peek.unsafe_chr.inspect} where an address should be")
    end
    nil
  end

  # Atoms, quoted strings and dots up to the next special.
  def phrase : Array(MailWord) | MailError
    words = [] of MailWord
    while true
      before = @scan.at
      problem = @scan.skip_cfws
      return problem if problem
      spaced = @scan.at > before && !words.empty?
      c = @scan.peek
      if c == 34_u8
        text = @scan.quoted
        return text if text.is_a?(MailError)
        words << MailWord.new(text, MailWord::QUOTED, spaced)
      elsif c == 46_u8
        @scan.advance
        words << MailWord.new(".", MailWord::DOT, spaced)
      elsif !@scan.done? && MailText.atext?(c)
        words << MailWord.new(@scan.atom, MailWord::ATOM, spaced)
      else
        break
      end
    end
    words
  end

  # The display name *words* spell: one space where whitespace was, none
  # between two encoded-words, each decoded.
  def self.display(words : Array(MailWord)) : String
    io = String::Builder.new(32)
    last_word = false
    words.each do |w|
      decoded = w.kind == MailWord::ATOM ? MailText.words_in(w.text, 0, w.text.bytesize) : nil
      io << " " if w.spaced && !(decoded && last_word)
      io << (decoded || w.text)
      last_word = !decoded.nil?
    end
    io.to_s
  end

  # `local@domain` at the cursor, or after the local part *phrase_words*
  # already read: the local part unquoted, and the domain.
  def addr_spec(phrase_words : Array(MailWord)?) : {String, String} | MailError
    words = phrase_words
    unless words
      read = phrase
      return read if read.is_a?(MailError)
      words = read
    end
    return fail("an address has no local part") if words.empty?
    local = String::Builder.new(32)
    want_word = true
    words.each do |w|
      if want_word
        return fail("a local part has a dot where a word should be") if w.kind == MailWord::DOT
        local << w.text
      else
        return fail("a local part has two words with no dot between them") unless w.kind == MailWord::DOT
        local << "."
      end
      want_word = !want_word
    end
    return fail("a local part ends in a dot") if want_word
    problem = @scan.skip_cfws
    return problem if problem
    return fail("an address has no @") unless @scan.peek == 64_u8
    @scan.advance
    problem = @scan.skip_cfws
    return problem if problem
    if @scan.peek == 91_u8
      lit = @scan.literal
      return lit if lit.is_a?(MailError)
      return {local.to_s, lit}
    end
    domain = String::Builder.new(32)
    while true
      label = @scan.atom
      return fail("a domain has an empty label") if label.empty?
      domain << label
      problem = @scan.skip_cfws
      return problem if problem
      if @scan.peek == 46_u8
        @scan.advance
        domain << "."
        problem = @scan.skip_cfws
        return problem if problem
      else
        break
      end
    end
    {local.to_s, domain.to_s}
  end
end

# One header line being written, folded at the spaces between its tokens.
class MailFolder
  LIMIT = 78

  @io : String::Builder
  @col : Int32
  @content : Bool

  def initialize(name : String)
    @io = String::Builder.new(128)
    @io << name << ":"
    @col = name.bytesize + 1
    @content = false
  end

  # *text*, never broken, after a space when *spaced*: on a line of its
  # own when it would carry this one past the limit. Nothing is folded
  # before the first token, which stays beside the name.
  def token(text : String, spaced : Bool = true) : Nil
    if spaced
      if @content && !text.empty? && @col + 1 + text.bytesize > LIMIT
        @io << "\r\n "
        @col = 1
      else
        @io << " "
        @col = @col + 1
      end
    end
    @io << text
    @col = @col + text.bytesize
    @content = true
  end

  # What an encoded-word after a space may take of this line: the rest
  # of it, and never more than section 2's 75.
  private def room : Int32
    left = LIMIT - @col - 1
    left > 75 ? 75 : left
  end

  # *text* as encoded-words, each after a space, filling the line before
  # folding to the next.
  def words(text : String) : Nil
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    q = MailText.q_size(p, 0, n) <= (n + 2) // 3 * 4
    i = 0
    while i < n
      j = MailText.word_end(p, i, n, room - 12, q)
      if j == i && @content
        @io << "\r\n"
        @col = 0
        j = MailText.word_end(p, i, n, room - 12, q)
      end
      j = i + MailText.char_size(p, i, n) if j == i
      word = MailText.word(text, i, j, q)
      @io << " " << word
      @col = @col + 1 + word.bytesize
      @content = true
      i = j
    end
  end

  # An unstructured value: as its words when they are printable ASCII
  # set apart by single spaces and each fits a line, encoded-words
  # otherwise.
  def unstructured(value : String) : Nil
    if MailFolder.plain?(value, @col)
      value.split(' ').each { |w| token(w) }
    else
      words(value)
    end
  end

  def self.plain?(value : String, col : Int32) : Bool
    return true if value.empty?
    return false if value.includes?("=?")
    p = value.to_unsafe
    n = value.bytesize
    return false if p[0] == 32_u8 || p[n - 1] == 32_u8
    word = 0
    first = true
    i = 0
    while i < n
      b = p[i]
      if b == 32_u8
        return false if word == 0
        first = false
        word = 0
      else
        return false if b < 33_u8 || b > 126_u8
        word = word + 1
        return false if word > LIMIT - 1 - (first ? col : 0)
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # A display name: atoms, a quoted string, or encoded-words.
  def phrase(name : String) : Nil
    p = name.to_unsafe
    n = name.bytesize
    atoms = !name.includes?("=?") && p[0] != 32_u8 && p[n - 1] != 32_u8
    printable = !name.includes?("=?") && n <= 70
    i = 0
    while i < n
      b = p[i]
      atoms = false unless (b < 128_u8 && MailText.atext?(b)) || (b == 32_u8 && i > 0 && p[i - 1] != 32_u8)
      printable = false unless b >= 32_u8 && b <= 126_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    if atoms
      name.split(' ').each { |w| token(w) }
    elsif printable
      token(MailText.quoted(name))
    else
      # Python's `email` reads a space into the gap between two
      # encoded-words of a phrase, where RFC 2047 section 6.2 drops it,
      # so two are put side by side only where nothing else will do: a
      # name that fits one encoded-word goes as one, on a line of its own
      # if need be, and a longer one of single-spaced words as atoms where
      # its words are atoms and encoded-words for the runs between them.
      if MailFolder.whole_word?(name)
        encoded(name)
      elsif atoms_apart?(name)
        run = [] of String
        name.split(' ').each do |w|
          if MailFolder.atom?(w)
            encoded(run.join(' ')) unless run.empty?
            run = [] of String
            token(w)
          else
            run << w
          end
        end
        encoded(run.join(' ')) unless run.empty?
      else
        words(name)
      end
    end
  end

  # *text* as one encoded-word when it fits one, as many as it needs
  # otherwise.
  private def encoded(text : String) : Nil
    if MailFolder.whole_word?(text)
      p = text.to_unsafe
      n = text.bytesize
      token(MailText.word(text, 0, n, MailText.q_size(p, 0, n) <= (n + 2) // 3 * 4))
    else
      words(text)
    end
  end

  def self.whole_word?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    q = MailText.q_size(p, 0, n) <= (n + 2) // 3 * 4
    MailText.word_end(p, 0, n, 63, q) == n
  end

  def self.atom?(word : String) : Bool
    return false if word.empty? || word.includes?("=?")
    p = word.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < word.bytesize
      return false unless p[i] < 128_u8 && MailText.atext?(p[i])
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # Whether *name* is words set apart by single spaces.
  private def atoms_apart?(name : String) : Bool
    !name.empty? && !name.starts_with?(" ") && !name.ends_with?(" ") && !name.includes?("  ") && !name.includes?("\t")
  end

  def finish : String
    @io << "\r\n"
    @io.to_s
  end
end

# A part as it is written: its header lines, and its body.
struct MailEntity
  getter head : String
  getter body : String

  def initialize(@head : String, @body : String)
  end

  def to_s : String
    @head + "\r\n" + @body
  end
end

pub struct Mail
  # How deep parts may nest before a message is refused.
  MAX_DEPTH = 32

  # A message read from *text*: its header section, then its body.
  def self.parse(text : String) : Part | MailError
    Part.parse(text)
  end

  # RFC 5322's date-time for *unix* seconds, at *offset* minutes east of
  # UTC: `Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0200`, as `email.utils.format_datetime`
  # writes it.
  def self.date(unix : Int64, offset : Int32 = 0) : String
    raise "mail: a Date's offset is within a day of UTC, -1439 to 1439 minutes, not #{offset}" if offset < -1439 || offset > 1439
    local = unix + offset.to_i64 * 60_i64
    # `//` and `%` truncate toward zero: a day before 1970 is floored here.
    days = local // 86400_i64
    days = days - 1_i64 if local % 86400_i64 < 0
    secs = (local - days * 86400_i64).to_i32
    civil = MailText.civil(days)
    raise "mail: a Date is in the years 1900 to 9999, not #{civil[0]}" if civil[0] < 1900 || civil[0] > 9999
    zone = offset < 0 ? -offset : offset
    "#{MailText::WEEKDAYS[((days % 7_i64 + 7_i64) % 7_i64).to_i32]}, #{MailText.two(civil[2])} #{MailText::MONTHS[civil[1] - 1]} #{civil[0]} " +
      "#{MailText.two(secs // 3600)}:#{MailText.two(secs // 60 % 60)}:#{MailText.two(secs % 60)} " +
      "#{offset < 0 ? "-" : "+"}#{MailText.two(zone // 60)}#{MailText.two(zone % 60)}"
  end

  # A date-time (RFC 5322 section 3.3, with section 4.3's obsolete years
  # and zones) as Unix seconds and the offset it was written at, in
  # minutes. The day of the week is not held to the date, as readers do
  # not hold it. A military zone is -0000, as section 4.3 says.
  def self.parse_date(text : String) : {Int64, Int32} | MailError
    bad = MailError.new("#{text.inspect} is not an RFC 5322 date")
    scan = MailScan.new(text)
    return bad if scan.skip_cfws
    name = scan.letters
    unless name.empty?
      return bad unless MailText::DAY_NAMES.includes?(name.downcase)
      return bad if scan.skip_cfws || scan.peek != 44_u8
      scan.advance
      return bad if scan.skip_cfws
    end
    day = scan.digits
    return bad if day.empty? || day.bytesize > 2 || scan.skip_cfws
    month = MailText::MONTHS.index(scan.letters.capitalize)
    return bad unless month
    return bad if scan.skip_cfws
    year_text = scan.digits
    return bad if year_text.bytesize < 2 || scan.skip_cfws
    year = year_text.to_i
    year = year + (year < 50 ? 2000 : 1900) if year_text.bytesize == 2
    year = year + 1900 if year_text.bytesize == 3
    hour = scan.digits
    return bad if hour.empty? || hour.bytesize > 2 || scan.skip_cfws || scan.peek != 58_u8
    scan.advance
    return bad if scan.skip_cfws
    minute = scan.digits
    return bad if minute.bytesize != 2 || scan.skip_cfws
    second = "0"
    if scan.peek == 58_u8
      scan.advance
      return bad if scan.skip_cfws
      second = scan.digits
      return bad if second.bytesize != 2 || scan.skip_cfws
    end
    offset = 0
    c = scan.peek
    if c == 43_u8 || c == 45_u8
      scan.advance
      zone = scan.digits
      return bad unless zone.bytesize == 4
      hh = zone[0, 2].to_i
      mm = zone[2, 2].to_i
      return bad if hh > 23 || mm > 59
      offset = (hh * 60 + mm) * (c == 45_u8 ? -1 : 1)
    else
      zone = scan.letters.downcase
      return MailError.new("#{text.inspect} has no zone") if zone.empty?
      known = MailText::ZONES[zone]?
      if known
        offset = known
      elsif zone.bytesize != 1 || zone == "j"
        return bad
      end
    end
    return bad if scan.skip_cfws || !scan.done?
    d = day.to_i
    h = hour.to_i
    mi = minute.to_i
    s = second.to_i
    return bad if year > 9999 || d < 1 || d > MailText.month_days(year, month + 1) || h > 23 || mi > 59 || s > 60
    days = MailText.days_from_civil(year, month + 1, d)
    {days * 86400_i64 + (h * 3600 + mi * 60 + s).to_i64 - offset.to_i64 * 60_i64, offset}
  end

  # An unstructured header line, `name: value` folded and encoded as the
  # builder writes it, CRLF and all.
  def self.fold(name : String, value : String) : String
    MailText.check_field(name, value)
    f = MailFolder.new(name)
    f.unstructured(value)
    f.finish
  end

  # *text* with its RFC 2047 encoded-words decoded.
  def self.decode_words(text : String) : String
    MailText.decode_words(text)
  end

  # *data* in base64, lines of 76 and a CRLF after each.
  def self.encode_base64(data : String) : String
    MailText.base64_encode(data)
  end

  # What base64 *text* stands for, characters outside the alphabet passed
  # over.
  def self.decode_base64(text : String) : String
    MailText.base64_decode(text)
  end

  # *data* in quoted-printable, lines of 76 or fewer; its line breaks
  # hard ones written as CRLF, or with *binary* escaped like any byte.
  def self.encode_quoted_printable(data : String, binary : Bool = false) : String
    MailText.qp_encode(data, binary)
  end

  # What quoted-printable *text* stands for, hard breaks as CRLF.
  def self.decode_quoted_printable(text : String) : String
    MailText.qp_decode(text)
  end

  # *text* from *charset* in UTF-8.
  def self.decode_charset(text : String, charset : String) : String | MailError
    MailText.decode(text, charset)
  end

  # A mailbox: an address and the name shown with it.
  class Address
    # The display name, decoded; "" when there is none.
    getter name : String
    # The local part, unquoted, and the domain as written.
    getter local : String
    getter domain : String

    # *address* is an addr-spec: `user@example.com`, or
    # `"john doe"@example.com` with its local part quoted. Anything else
    # is a panic, as is a name with a line break in it.
    def initialize(address : String, @name : String = "")
      raise "mail: a display name holds a line break" if MailText.line_break?(@name)
      raise "mail: a display name is not UTF-8" unless MailText.utf8?(@name)
      reader = MailAddresses.new(address)
      spec = reader.addr_spec(phrase_words: nil)
      raise "mail: #{address.inspect} is not an address: #{spec.reason}" if spec.is_a?(MailError)
      @local = spec[0]
      @domain = spec[1]
    end

    # What `parse` makes, already read.
    def initialize(@local : String, @domain : String, @name : String, read : Bool)
    end

    # The addr-spec: the local part quoted where it is not a dot-atom.
    def address : String
      MailText.quote_local(@local) + "@" + @domain
    end

    # As a person reads it: `Name <user@example.com>`, the name quoted
    # when it is not atoms, nothing encoded.
    def to_s : String
      return address if @name.empty?
      plain = true
      p = @name.to_unsafe
      i = 0
      while i < @name.bytesize
        plain = false unless MailText.atext?(p[i]) || p[i] == 32_u8
        i = i + 1
      end
      (plain ? @name : MailText.quoted(@name)) + " <" + address + ">"
    end

    # Whether the address is more than ASCII (RFC 6532), which only a
    # server that offers SMTPUTF8 takes.
    def utf8? : Bool
      !MailText.ascii?(@local) || !MailText.ascii?(@domain)
    end

    def ==(other : Address) : Bool
      @name == other.name && @local == other.local && @domain == other.domain
    end

    # The one mailbox *text* holds.
    def self.parse(text : String) : Address | MailError
      list = Address.parse_list(text)
      return list if list.is_a?(MailError)
      return MailError.new("#{text.inspect} holds #{list.size} addresses, not one") unless list.size == 1
      list[0]
    end

    # The mailboxes of an address list, a group's members in its place.
    def self.parse_list(text : String) : Array(Address) | MailError
      MailAddresses.new(text).list
    end
  end

  # A message to be written: its addresses, subject, date and identifier,
  # its text and HTML, and its attachments.
  class Message
    property from : Address? = nil
    getter to : Array(Address) = [] of Address
    getter cc : Array(Address) = [] of Address
    getter bcc : Array(Address) = [] of Address
    getter reply_to : Array(Address) = [] of Address
    @subject : String? = nil
    @date : String? = nil
    @message_id : String? = nil
    @text : String? = nil
    @html : String? = nil
    # File name, media type, bytes.
    @attachments : Array({String, String, String}) = [] of {String, String, String}
    @extra : Array({String, String}) = [] of {String, String}

    OWN = ["from", "to", "cc", "bcc", "reply-to", "subject", "date", "message-id", "mime-version"]

    def initialize
    end

    def subject : String?
      @subject
    end

    def subject=(value : String) : String
      MailText.check_field("Subject", value)
      @subject = value
    end

    def date : String?
      @date
    end

    # An RFC 5322 date-time - `Mail.date` writes one - refused when it is
    # not one.
    def date=(value : String) : String
      MailText.check_field("Date", value)
      raise "mail: the Date #{value.inspect} is not an RFC 5322 date" if Mail.parse_date(value).is_a?(MailError)
      @date = value
    end

    def message_id : String?
      @message_id
    end

    # `<left@right>`, the brackets added when they are not there.
    def message_id=(value : String) : String
      MailText.check_field("Message-ID", value)
      id = value.starts_with?("<") && value.ends_with?(">") ? value[1, value.bytesize - 2] : value
      at = id.index('@')
      ok = !at.nil? && at > 0 && at < id.bytesize - 1
      p = id.to_unsafe
      i = 0
      while ok && i < id.bytesize
        b = p[i]
        ok = false if b <= 32_u8 || b >= 127_u8 || b == 60_u8 || b == 62_u8 || b == 34_u8 || b == 92_u8
        i = i + 1
      end
      raise "mail: the Message-ID #{value.inspect} is not <left@right>" unless ok
      @message_id = "<" + id + ">"
    end

    def text : String?
      @text
    end

    def text=(value : String) : String
      raise "mail: the text is not UTF-8" unless MailText.utf8?(value)
      @text = value
    end

    def html : String?
      @html
    end

    def html=(value : String) : String
      raise "mail: the HTML is not UTF-8" unless MailText.utf8?(value)
      @html = value
    end

    # A file sent with the message, under *filename*, as *content_type*:
    # the type its extension names (std/mime) when nil.
    def attach(filename : String, data : String, content_type : String? = nil) : self
      raise "mail: an attachment's file name holds a line break" if MailText.line_break?(filename)
      raise "mail: an attachment's file name is not UTF-8" unless MailText.utf8?(filename)
      raise "mail: an attachment's file name is empty" if filename.empty?
      kind = content_type || MIME.from_extension(filename)
      raise "mail: #{kind.inspect} is not a media type" if MailText.line_break?(kind) || MailText.content_type(kind).nil? || !MailText.ascii?(kind)
      @attachments << {filename, kind, data}
      self
    end

    # A header of the caller's own, written as unstructured text. The
    # headers the message writes itself are refused here.
    def header(name : String, value : String) : self
      MailText.check_field(name, value)
      lower = name.downcase
      raise "mail: #{name} is set through the message's own property" if OWN.includes?(lower) || lower.starts_with?("content-")
      @extra << {name, value}
      self
    end

    # Every address the message goes to - To, Cc and Bcc - once each.
    def recipients : Array(String)
      seen = [] of String
      (@to + @cc + @bcc).each do |a|
        seen << a.address unless seen.includes?(a.address)
      end
      seen
    end

    # Whether an address in the message is RFC 6532's UTF-8.
    def utf8? : Bool
      sender = @from
      return true if sender && sender.utf8?
      (@to + @cc + @bcc + @reply_to).any? { |a| a.utf8? }
    end

    # The message, CRLF line breaks and all.
    def to_s : String
      sender = @from
      raise "mail: a message has no From" unless sender
      date = @date
      raise "mail: a message has no Date" unless date
      head = String::Builder.new(1024)
      head << Mail.fold("Date", date)
      head << Message.addresses("From", [sender])
      head << Message.addresses("Reply-To", @reply_to) unless @reply_to.empty?
      head << Message.addresses("To", @to) unless @to.empty?
      head << Message.addresses("Cc", @cc) unless @cc.empty?
      if subject = @subject
        head << Mail.fold("Subject", subject)
      end
      if id = @message_id
        head << "Message-ID: " << id << "\r\n"
      end
      @extra.each { |pair| head << Mail.fold(pair[0], pair[1]) }
      head << "MIME-Version: 1.0\r\n"
      entity = content
      head << entity.head << "\r\n" << entity.body
      head.to_s
    end

    protected def self.addresses(name : String, list : Array(Address)) : String
      f = MailFolder.new(name)
      list.each_with_index do |a, i|
        tail = i == list.size - 1 ? "" : ","
        if a.name.empty?
          f.token(a.address + tail)
        else
          f.phrase(a.name)
          f.token("<" + a.address + ">" + tail)
        end
      end
      f.finish
    end

    private def content : MailEntity
      body = nil.as(MailEntity?)
      text = @text
      html = @html
      if text && html
        body = Message.multipart("alternative", [Message.text_part("plain", text), Message.text_part("html", html)])
      elsif html
        body = Message.text_part("html", html)
      elsif text || @attachments.empty?
        body = Message.text_part("plain", text || "")
      end
      return body if body && @attachments.empty?
      children = [] of MailEntity
      children << body if body
      @attachments.each { |item| children << Message.attachment(item[0], item[1], item[2]) }
      Message.multipart("mixed", children)
    end

    protected def self.text_part(subtype : String, text : String) : MailEntity
      canonical = MailText.crlf(text)
      encoding = "7bit"
      body = canonical
      unless Message.seven_bit?(canonical)
        qp = MailText.qp_encode(canonical, false)
        b64 = MailText.base64_encode(canonical)
        encoding = qp.bytesize <= b64.bytesize ? "quoted-printable" : "base64"
        body = qp.bytesize <= b64.bytesize ? qp : b64
      end
      head = "Content-Type: text/#{subtype}; charset=\"utf-8\"\r\nContent-Transfer-Encoding: #{encoding}\r\n"
      MailEntity.new(head, body)
    end

    # ASCII without NUL, in lines of 78 or fewer.
    protected def self.seven_bit?(text : String) : Bool
      p = text.to_unsafe
      col = 0
      i = 0
      while i < text.bytesize
        b = p[i]
        return false if b == 0_u8 || b >= 128_u8
        if b == 13_u8
          col = 0
        elsif b != 10_u8
          col = col + 1
          return false if col > MailFolder::LIMIT
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
      true
    end

    protected def self.attachment(filename : String, kind : String, data : String) : MailEntity
      type = MailFolder.new("Content-Type")
      kind.split(' ').each { |w| type.token(w) }
      disposition = MailFolder.new("Content-Disposition")
      disposition.token("attachment;")
      Message.filename_params(filename).each { |param| disposition.token(param) }
      head = type.finish + disposition.finish + "Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64\r\n"
      MailEntity.new(head, MailText.base64_encode(data))
    end

    # `filename="..."` for a short name of printable ASCII; RFC 2231's
    # `filename*=utf-8''...` for any other, in continuations of whole
    # characters when it is longer - 50 encoded bytes in the first, after
    # its charset, and 60 in the rest, so each fits a line. All but the
    # last end in `;`.
    protected def self.filename_params(filename : String) : Array(String)
      p = filename.to_unsafe
      n = filename.bytesize
      simple = n <= 60 && !filename.includes?("=?")
      i = 0
      while simple && i < n
        b = p[i]
        simple = false if b < 32_u8 || b > 126_u8 || b == 34_u8 || b == 92_u8
        i = i + 1
      end
      return ["filename=" + MailText.quoted(filename)] if simple
      chunks = [] of String
      chunk = String::Builder.new(64)
      size = 0
      i = 0
      while i < n
        c = MailText.char_size(p, i, n)
        piece = String::Builder.new(12)
        k = i
        while k < i + c
          if MailText.attr_char?(p[k])
            piece.write_byte(p[k])
          else
            MailText.escape(piece, p[k], 37_u8)
          end
          k = k + 1
        end
        text = piece.to_s
        if size + text.bytesize > (chunks.empty? ? 50 : 60) && size > 0
          chunks << chunk.to_s
          chunk = String::Builder.new(64)
          size = 0
        end
        chunk << text
        size = size + text.bytesize
        i = i + c
      end
      chunks << chunk.to_s
      if chunks.size == 1 && chunks[0].bytesize <= 52
        return ["filename*=utf-8''" + chunks[0]]
      end
      params = [] of String
      chunks.each_with_index do |text, k|
        prefix = k == 0 ? "utf-8''" : ""
        params << "filename*#{k}*=" + prefix + text + (k == chunks.size - 1 ? "" : ";")
      end
      params
    end

    # The parts under one boundary, made from their own hash.
    protected def self.multipart(subtype : String, children : Array(MailEntity)) : MailEntity
      rendered = children.map { |c| c.to_s }
      digest = SHA1.new
      rendered.each { |r| digest.update(r) }
      mark = "iyi-" + digest.hexfinal[0, 32]
      while rendered.any? { |r| ("\r\n" + r).includes?("\r\n--" + mark) }
        mark = "iyi-" + SHA1.hexdigest(mark)[0, 32]
      end
      body = String::Builder.new(1024)
      rendered.each_with_index do |r, i|
        body << (i == 0 ? "--" : "\r\n--") << mark << "\r\n" << r
      end
      body << "\r\n--" << mark << "--\r\n"
      head = "Content-Type: multipart/#{subtype};\r\n boundary=\"#{mark}\"\r\n"
      MailEntity.new(head, body.to_s)
    end
  end

  # A message or one of its parts, as read: its header fields unfolded and
  # in order, and its body - a multipart's parts, a `message/rfc822`'s
  # message, or bytes in a transfer encoding.
  class Part
    # Each field's name as written and its value unfolded, whitespace at
    # either end dropped, nothing decoded.
    getter headers : Array({String, String})
    # The parts of a multipart, or the message a `message/rfc822` holds.
    getter parts : Array(Part)
    # `type/subtype` in lower case: `text/plain` when the part names none,
    # or names one that cannot be read (RFC 2045 section 5.2).
    getter media_type : String
    @params : Hash(String, String)
    @source : String
    @body_at : Int32
    @body_end : Int32

    def initialize(@source : String, @headers : Array({String, String}), @media_type : String, @params : Hash(String, String), @body_at : Int32, @body_end : Int32, @parts : Array(Part))
    end

    def self.parse(text : String) : Part | MailError
      Part.read(text, 0, text.bytesize, 0, "text/plain")
    end

    protected def self.read(source : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32, depth : Int32, default_type : String) : Part | MailError
      return MailError.new("parts are nested deeper than #{MAX_DEPTH}") if depth > MAX_DEPTH
      p = source.to_unsafe
      headers = [] of {String, String}
      name = nil.as(String?)
      value = String::Builder.new(64)
      body_at = stop
      line = 0
      i = start
      while i < stop
        j = i
        while j < stop && p[j] != 10_u8
          j = j + 1
        end
        e = j
        e = e - 1 if e > i && p[e - 1] == 13_u8
        after = j < stop ? j + 1 : stop
        line = line + 1
        if e == i
          body_at = after
          break
        end
        if MailText.wsp?(p[i])
          return MailError.new("the header section begins with a folded line") unless name
          value.write(p + i, e - i)
        else
          if field = name
            headers << {field, Part.trim(value.to_s)}
          end
          colon = i
          while colon < e && p[colon] != 58_u8
            colon = colon + 1
          end
          stem = colon
          while stem > i && MailText.wsp?(p[stem - 1])
            stem = stem - 1
          end
          field = MailText.cut(source, i, stem)
          unless colon < e && MailText.field_name?(field)
            shown = MailText.cut(source, i, e)
            shown = shown[0, 60] + "..." if shown.bytesize > 64
            return MailError.new("line #{line} of a header section is not a field: #{shown.inspect}")
          end
          name = field
          value = String::Builder.new(64)
          value.write(p + colon + 1, e - colon - 1)
        end
        body_at = after
        i = after
      end
      if field = name
        headers << {field, Part.trim(value.to_s)}
      end
      media = default_type
      params = {} of String => String
      if type = Part.first(headers, "content-type")
        parsed = MailText.content_type(type)
        if parsed
          media = parsed[0]
          params = parsed[1]
        else
          media = "text/plain"
        end
      end
      parts = [] of Part
      if media.starts_with?("multipart/")
        boundary = params["boundary"]?
        return MailError.new("a #{media} part names no boundary") unless boundary && !boundary.empty?
        ranges = Part.split(source, body_at, stop, boundary, media)
        return ranges if ranges.is_a?(MailError)
        child_type = media == "multipart/digest" ? "message/rfc822" : "text/plain"
        ranges.each do |range|
          child = Part.read(source, range[0], range[1], depth + 1, child_type)
          return child if child.is_a?(MailError)
          parts << child
        end
      elsif media == "message/rfc822"
        cte = (Part.first(headers, "content-transfer-encoding") || "7bit").strip.downcase
        if cte == "7bit" || cte == "8bit" || cte == "binary"
          inner = Part.read(source, body_at, stop, depth + 1, "text/plain")
          return inner if inner.is_a?(MailError)
          parts << inner
        end
      end
      Part.new(source, headers, media, params, body_at, stop, parts)
    end

    protected def self.trim(value : String) : String
      p = value.to_unsafe
      a = 0
      b = value.bytesize
      while a < b && MailText.wsp?(p[a])
        a = a + 1
      end
      while b > a && MailText.wsp?(p[b - 1])
        b = b - 1
      end
      a == 0 && b == value.bytesize ? value : MailText.cut(value, a, b)
    end

    protected def self.first(headers : Array({String, String}), lower : String) : String?
      headers.each do |pair|
        return pair[1] if pair[0].downcase == lower
      end
      nil
    end

    # The parts of a multipart body: between `--boundary` lines, the line
    # break before each line its own; to the `--boundary--` line. The
    # preamble and the epilogue are passed over.
    protected def self.split(source : String, start : Int32, stop : Int32, boundary : String, media : String) : Array({Int32, Int32}) | MailError
      p = source.to_unsafe
      mark = "--" + boundary
      m = mark.to_unsafe
      size = mark.bytesize
      ranges = [] of {Int32, Int32}
      open_at = -1
      i = start
      while i < stop
        j = i
        while j < stop && p[j] != 10_u8
          j = j + 1
        end
        e = j
        e = e - 1 if e > i && p[e - 1] == 13_u8
        if e - i >= size && p[i] == 45_u8 && p[i + 1] == 45_u8 && (i + size <= stop) && Part.prefix?(p + i, m, size)
          k = i + size
          closing = k + 2 <= e && p[k] == 45_u8 && p[k + 1] == 45_u8
          rest = closing ? k + 2 : k
          blank = true
          while rest < e
            blank = false unless MailText.wsp?(p[rest])
            rest = rest + 1
          end
          if blank
            if open_at >= 0
              stop_at = i
              if stop_at > open_at
                stop_at = stop_at - 1
                stop_at = stop_at - 1 if stop_at > open_at && p[stop_at - 1] == 13_u8
              end
              ranges << {open_at, stop_at}
            end
            return ranges if closing && open_at >= 0
            return MailError.new("a #{media} body closes before its first part") if closing
            open_at = j < stop ? j + 1 : stop
          end
        end
        i = j + 1
      end
      return MailError.new("a #{media} body holds no line --#{boundary}") if open_at < 0
      MailError.new("a #{media} body ends before its closing line --#{boundary}--")
    end

    protected def self.prefix?(a : Pointer(UInt8), b : Pointer(UInt8), size : Int32) : Bool
      k = 0
      while k < size
        return false unless a[k] == b[k]
        k = k + 1
      end
      true
    end

    # The first field named *name*, any case, its encoded-words decoded.
    def header(name : String) : String?
      raw = raw_header(name)
      raw ? MailText.decode_words(raw) : nil
    end

    # Every field named *name*, in order, decoded.
    def header_values(name : String) : Array(String)
      lower = name.downcase
      found = [] of String
      @headers.each do |pair|
        found << MailText.decode_words(pair[1]) if pair[0].downcase == lower
      end
      found
    end

    # The first field named *name*, as written.
    def raw_header(name : String) : String?
      Part.first(@headers, name.downcase)
    end

    def subject : String?
      header("Subject")
    end

    # The mailboxes of every field named *name* - `From`, `To`, `Cc` -
    # none when there is no such field.
    def addresses(name : String) : Array(Address) | MailError
      lower = name.downcase
      found = [] of Address
      @headers.each do |pair|
        if pair[0].downcase == lower
          list = Address.parse_list(pair[1])
          return list if list.is_a?(MailError)
          found = found + list
        end
      end
      found
    end

    # A Content-Type parameter, RFC 2231's decoded.
    def param(name : String) : String?
      @params[name.downcase]?
    end

    # The charset text is in: `us-ascii` when none is named (RFC 2045
    # section 5.2), in lower case.
    def charset : String
      (@params["charset"]? || "us-ascii").downcase
    end

    # The disposition in lower case - `attachment`, `inline` - or nil.
    def disposition : String?
      raw = raw_header("Content-Disposition")
      return nil unless raw
      parsed = MailText.disposition(raw)
      parsed ? parsed[0] : nil
    end

    # The file name of Content-Disposition, or else Content-Type's `name`.
    def filename : String?
      if raw = raw_header("Content-Disposition")
        parsed = MailText.disposition(raw)
        if parsed
          name = parsed[1]["filename"]?
          return name if name
        end
      end
      @params["name"]?
    end

    def attachment? : Bool
      disposition == "attachment"
    end

    def multipart? : Bool
      @media_type.starts_with?("multipart/")
    end

    # The transfer encoding in lower case: `7bit` when none is named.
    def transfer_encoding : String
      (raw_header("Content-Transfer-Encoding") || "7bit").strip.downcase
    end

    # The bytes the body stands for, its transfer encoding undone.
    def body : String | MailError
      raw = MailText.cut(@source, @body_at, @body_end)
      case encoding = transfer_encoding
      when "7bit", "8bit", "binary"
        raw
      when "base64"
        MailText.base64_decode(raw)
      when "quoted-printable"
        MailText.qp_decode(raw)
      else
        MailError.new("the Content-Transfer-Encoding #{encoding.inspect} is not one std/mail reads")
      end
    end

    # The body as text in UTF-8, from the part's charset, each CRLF an LF.
    def text : String | MailError
      bytes = body
      return bytes if bytes.is_a?(MailError)
      decoded = MailText.decode(bytes, charset)
      return decoded if decoded.is_a?(MailError)
      MailText.lf(decoded)
    end

    # This part, then each under it, depth first.
    def walk(&block : Part -> Nil) : Nil
      block.call(self)
      @parts.each { |child| child.walk(&block) }
    end

    # The first part of *media_type* that is not an attachment, depth
    # first: `find("text/plain")` is the text a reader shows.
    def find(media_type : String) : Part?
      wanted = media_type.downcase
      found = nil.as(Part?)
      walk do |part|
        found = part if found.nil? && part.media_type == wanted && !part.attachment?
      end
      found
    end

    # The parts whose disposition is `attachment`, in order.
    def attachments : Array(Part)
      found = [] of Part
      walk { |part| found << part if part.attachment? }
      found
    end
  end
end
